---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/hgb/drs19.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  subtitle={metadata.nativeTitle}
  framework="hgb"
  status={metadata.status}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Scope" },        { id: "s2", label: "Control tests" },        { id: "s3", label: "Exclusion grounds" },        { id: "s4", label: "Worked example" },        { id: "s5", label: "IFRS" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Scope — the Foundational "Who's In the Group" Question</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card info span2">
<div class="ctitle">Elaborating §290's own control tests and §296's own exclusion grounds</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 19 sits at the very foundation of group accounting: it elaborates HOW to apply the §290 HGB control tests (established in detail in the earlier HGB poster series — voting rights, appointment rights, control agreements, and the structured-entity/SPE risks-and-rewards test) and the §296 exclusion grounds in genuinely ambiguous, real-world scenarios the bare statutory text doesn't fully resolve on its own.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Applying the Control Tests to Genuinely Ambiguous Structures</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="cols-1">
<div class="card">
<div class="ctitle">Beyond simple majority ownership</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 19 provides detailed guidance for scenarios where control is genuinely NOT obvious from ownership percentage alone — potential voting rights (options, convertible instruments), de facto control through consistently-achieved voting majorities at general meetings even without formal majority ownership, and structured entities where the §290(2) Nr.4 risks-and-rewards test applies rather than a traditional voting-rights analysis.</div>
</div>
<div class="card">
<div class="ctitle">Special-purpose entities, elaborated</div>
<div class="principle">Given HGB's own SPE-capture mechanism (§290(2) Nr.4, established in the earlier HGB poster series as parallelling IFRS 10's own structured-entity approach) is deliberately principles-based rather than a bright-line test, DRS 19 supplies worked criteria and indicators for determining when an entity genuinely bears the majority of an SPE's own risks and rewards.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Exclusion Grounds, Applied</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card warn span2">
<div class="ctitle">Genuine guidance for the narrow §296 exceptions</div>
<div class="principle">Recall the four narrow §296(1) exclusion grounds established in the earlier HGB poster series (severe restrictions, disproportionate cost, held exclusively for resale, subordinate importance) — DRS 19 provides detailed guidance on evidencing each ground, and specifically elaborates the §296(2) anti-abuse aggregation rule (multiple individually-immaterial subsidiaries still requiring inclusion if collectively material), a genuinely important safeguard against a group deliberately excluding several smaller entities piecemeal.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Worked Example — De Facto Control Without Majority Ownership</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card span2">
<div class="worked">
<div class="wk-title">A 35% shareholder consistently controlling the general meeting</div>
<div class="wk-body">A company holds 35% of another entity's voting shares — below the formal §290(2) Nr.1 majority threshold — but the remaining shares are widely dispersed among thousands of small shareholders who consistently do not attend general meetings, meaning the 35% stake has, in every meeting over the past several years, effectively controlled the outcome of every shareholder vote.</div>
<div class="wk-answer">DRS 19 provides guidance that this "de facto control" pattern — consistent, demonstrated voting dominance despite formal minority ownership — CAN satisfy §290's own control test, requiring full consolidation, even though a purely mechanical percentage-ownership test would suggest otherwise.</div>
</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 10)</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card span2">
<div class="ctitle">A genuinely strong convergence in underlying philosophy</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 19's own approach to de facto control and structured entities closely mirrors IFRS 10's own control model — both frameworks have moved toward a substance-based, principles-driven control assessment rather than a purely mechanical voting-percentage test, reflecting a broadly shared international consensus that genuine economic control, however achieved, should drive consolidation scope.</div>
</div>
</div>
</StandardPageLayout>
